# Bring stone within mining reach by digging where the body stands. In flat
# terrain stone lies three to six blocks under the dirt and no ray ever reaches
# it from the surface, so `dig_down` cuts a one-wide staircase down until the
# stone is known within mining reach and returns confirmed with the block in
# reach of `mine`. A person with a pickaxe digs; walking the surface is the
# fallback, not the first move.
#
# When the ground under the body refuses the stair (a drop beside it, water),
# `dig_down` steps a few blocks aside to a cell it can dig from and starts
# there, at most twice per call; only when no such cell lies within five blocks
# does it fail, so a refusal here means the ground around is not diggable. The
# refusal is deterministic, so the two retries the per-rule cap allows fail in
# the same instant: the frame ends after a few ticks, not a long wait.
#
# It is a skill of its own for two reasons. A failed dig is ONE failure of the
# caller's rule (three failed digs inside this frame, the per-rule cap, fail it
# with the dig's kind), and a CHILD frame is the only way a caller reaches the
# rule below on the same tick: a failed primitive retries in place on the next
# tick and never falls through, so the dig has to be called as a skill. See
# get_cobblestone: the surface search sits underneath this frame and fires on
# the tick this one failed.
#
# It fails with the dig's own kind (`fluid`, `unsafe_drop`, `too_deep`,
# `no_block`, `needs_tool`, `stuck`), so a caller must not read every failure as
# "keep searching": a stair refused because it would open water or because the
# floor of the next step is a drop, at the body's cell and at every cell within
# five blocks of it, means this ground has no safe stair, not that stone is
# absent. The caller decides what to try next.
skill find_stone()
  doc "Dig a staircase down to the stone under the body; fails with the dig's own kind when the ground refuses it."
  rules:
    near(stone, 5) -> done
    not near(stone, 5) -> dig_down(stone)
